 ## Overview
-GPU Instancing with Indirect Draw Calls is a technique to render a large number of identical meshes (voxels) efficiently by using indirect draw calls [S1, unofficial]. Instead of issuing one draw call per instance, a single draw call is issued that reads instance counts and parameters from a GPU-accessible buffer, allowing the GPU to generate the draw commands itself [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by decoupling the CPU-side draw call issuance from the GPU-side execution logic, enabling dynamic instance counts and reducing CPU-GPU synchronization overhead compared to standard hardware instancing [S1, unofficial].
+GPU Instancing with Indirect Draw Calls is a technique to render a large number of identical meshes (voxels) efficiently by using indirect draw calls [S1, unofficial]. Instead of issuing one draw call per instance, a single draw call is issued that reads instance counts and parameters from a GPU-accessible buffer, allowing the GPU to generate the draw commands itself [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by decoupling CPU-side draw call issuance from GPU-side execution, enabling dynamic instance counts and reducing CPU-GPU synchronization overhead compared to standard hardware instancing [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A single mesh geometry (e.g., a voxel cube), a material with procedural instancing support, and a buffer containing instance data (positions, occupancy) and indirect draw arguments.
-*   **Output:** Rasterized voxels on the screen.
+*   **Input:** A single mesh geometry (e.g., a voxel cube), a material with procedural instancing support, and a buffer containing instance data (positions, occupancy) and indirect draw arguments [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** Rasterized voxels on the screen [S1, unofficial].
 
